/**


@page update-wheel Wheel Odometry Update

Ground vehicles often move in a plane at near constant velocity or acceleration, which is a motion that leaves a monocular visual-inertial system poorly observable (e.g. its scale).
Wheel readings are available on most of them, and can be fused to address this.
The update here follows the visual-inertial-wheel odometry of Lee et al. @cite Lee2020IROS : the wheel readings between the two newest clones are integrated into a single 2D relative pose, which then updates these two clones.
The wheel intrinsics, the wheel to IMU extrinsics and the time offset between them can be calibrated online.
It is disabled by default (see `use_wheel`), and is fed through ov_msckf::VioManager::feed_measurement_wheel().


@section update-wheel-meas Preintegrated Measurement

The odometry frame \f$\{O\}\f$ has its x-axis forward, y-axis left and z-axis up, and the wheels move it along its x-axis only.
For a differential drive with left and right wheel angular velocities \f$\omega_l,\omega_r\f$, wheel radii \f$r_l,r_r\f$ and baseline \f$b\f$ we have:

\f{align*}{
    {}^O\omega &= (\omega_r r_r - \omega_l r_l) / b \\
    {}^Ov &= (\omega_r r_r + \omega_l r_l) / 2
\f}

Integrating the 2D kinematics from the clone time \f$t_k\f$ to \f$t_{k+1}\f$ gives the relative yaw and position of the odometry frame:

\f{align*}{
    \mathbf{z}_{k+1} =
    \begin{bmatrix} {}^{O_{k+1}}_{O_k}\theta \\ {}^{O_k}\mathbf{d}_{O_{k+1}} \end{bmatrix} =
    \begin{bmatrix}
    \int {}^{O_t}\omega ~dt \\
    \int {}^{O_t}v \cos({}^{O_t}_{O_k}\theta) ~dt \\
    \int {}^{O_t}v \sin({}^{O_t}_{O_k}\theta) ~dt
    \end{bmatrix}
\f}

The covariance of this measurement and its Jacobian in respect to the intrinsics \f$[r_l~r_r~b]\f$ are integrated along with it, one reading at a time.
The state predicts it through the two clones and the extrinsics \f$\{{}^O_I\mathbf{R},{}^O\mathbf{p}_I\}\f$:

\f{align*}{
    {}^{O_{k+1}}_{O_k}\theta &= \mathbf{e}_3^\top \mathrm{Log}({}^O_I\mathbf{R} {}^{I_{k+1}}_G\mathbf{R} {}^{I_k}_G\mathbf{R}^\top {}^O_I\mathbf{R}^\top) \\
    {}^{O_k}\mathbf{d}_{O_{k+1}} &= \Lambda \Big( {}^O_I\mathbf{R} {}^{I_k}_G\mathbf{R} ({}^G\mathbf{p}_{I_{k+1}} + {}^{I_{k+1}}_G\mathbf{R}^\top {}^I\mathbf{p}_O - {}^G\mathbf{p}_{I_k}) + {}^O\mathbf{p}_I \Big)
\f}

where \f$\Lambda = [\mathbf{e}_1~\mathbf{e}_2]^\top\f$.
The wheel readings are integrated over the clone times shifted by the current estimate of the time offset \f${}^Ot_I\f$ (\f$t_{imu} = t_{wheel} + {}^Ot_I\f$), and an error in it moves both poses along their velocities, which gives its Jacobian.
The update is rejected if it fails a chi-squared test.


@section update-wheel-types Measurement Types

- `Wheel2DAng` - left and right wheel angular velocities (rad/s). The intrinsics are the left radius, right radius and baseline.
- `Wheel2DLin` - left and right wheel linear velocities (m/s). The intrinsics are the left scale, right scale and baseline.
- `Wheel2DCen` - angular (rad/s) and linear (m/s) velocity of the odometry frame. There are no intrinsics.


@section update-wheel-model Where the Kinematic Model Does Not Hold

The differential drive model assumes that the wheels do not slip and that the motion is planar between two clones.
On a skid-steering vehicle the wheel speed difference is a poor yaw source, thus the yaw of the measurement can be left out (`wheel_use_yaw`) so that only the displacement is used.
Slip and non-planar motion can be modeled with additional measurement noise: a constant one (`wheel_noise_floor_yaw`, `wheel_noise_floor_x`, `wheel_noise_floor_y`) and one that grows with how much the vehicle turned between the two clones (`wheel_noise_turn`).


@section update-wheel-degenerate Degenerate Motions

As analyzed in @cite Lee2020IROS the calibration parameters are not observable under all motions:

| Motion | Unobservable |
|---|---|
| Pure translation | \f${}^O\mathbf{p}_I\f$, \f$b\f$ |
| 1-axis rotation | \f${}^O\mathbf{p}_I\f$ (along the rotation axis) |
| Constant angular and linear velocity | \f${}^Ot_I\f$ |
| No left / right wheel velocity | \f$r_l\f$ / \f$r_r\f$ |
| No motion | all |

Only calibrate what the expected motion excites, and give the rest a good prior.


*/
